</math> is the speed, in miles per hour, of a certain car <math alttext="t"><mi>t</mi>
</math> seconds after it began to accelerate. Therefore, the speed of the car, in miles per hour, <math alttext="5"><mn>5</mn>
</math> seconds after it began to accelerate can be found by substituting <math alttext="5"><mn>5</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="t"><mi>t</mi>
</math> in the given equation, which yields&nbsp;<math alttext="s equals 40 plus 3 left parenthesis 5 right parenthesis"><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mn>5</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext="s equals 55"><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>55</mn></math>. Thus, the speed of the car <math alttext="5"><mn>5</mn>
</math> seconds after it began to accelerate is <math alttext="55"><mn>55</mn>
</math> miles per hour.</p>
<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
