</math>-pound boxes are in the shipment?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"673333a3-8cba-4df2-b54c-9e41063e91ad","content":"<p><math alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"cef395a6-10b1-461e-8a05-ba36fbcf0cd3","content":"<p><math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"ee3ba520-eee6-40db-837a-1088cb92ae05","content":"<p><math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"2dce2764-3771-4483-9006-f4b1a208986a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2dce2764-3771-4483-9006-f4b1a208986a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is correct. It's given that the shipment consists of <math alttext="5"><mn>5</mn>
</math>-pound boxes and <math alttext="10"><mn>10</mn>
</math>-pound boxes with a total weight of <math alttext="220"><mn>220</mn>
</math> pounds. Let <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> represent the number of <math alttext="5"><mn>5</mn>
</math>-pound boxes and <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math> represent the number of <math alttext="10"><mn>10</mn>
</math>-pound boxes in the shipment. Therefore, the equation <math alttext="5 x plus 10 y equals 220"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>5</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mrow>
			<mn>10</mn>
			<mi>y</mi>
		</mrow>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>220</mn>
</mrow>
</math> represents this situation. It's given that there are <math alttext="13"><mn>13</mn>
</math> <math alttext="10"><mn>10</mn>
</math>-pound boxes in the shipment. Substituting <math alttext="13"><mn>13</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math> in the equation <math alttext="5 x plus 10 y equals 220"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>5</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mrow>
			<mn>10</mn>
			<mi>y</mi>
		</mrow>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>220</mn>
</mrow>
</math> yields <math alttext="5 x plus 10 left parenthesis 13 right parenthesis equals 220"><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mfenced><mn>13</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>220</mn></math>, or <math alttext="5 x plus 130 equals 220"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>5</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mn>130</mn>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>220</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Subtracting <math alttext="130"><mn>130</mn>
</math> from both sides of this equation yields <math alttext="5 x equals 90"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mn>5</mn>
		<mi>x</mi>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>90</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="5"><mn>5</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="18"><mn>18</mn>
</math>. Thus, there are <math alttext="18"><mn>18</mn>
</math> <math alttext="5"><mn>5</mn>
</math>-pound boxes in the shipment.</p>
